First Author: Hickey
Title: Long-Term outcomes in adult patients with congenital heart disease considered for transplantation: A single center study
Source number: 18

Very short summary: This single-center retrospective study evaluated adult congenital heart disease patients considered for heart or heart-plus-organ transplantation and used a machine-learning decision tree to identify predictors of 1- and 10-year survival. Five key findings were: (1) 58 ACHD patients were included; (2) the most common diagnoses were D-TGA with atrial switch, tetralogy of Fallot/pulmonary atresia, and tricuspid atresia; (3) invasive hemodynamic variables, especially PCWP, SVP, and EDP, were the strongest correlates of outcomes; (4) transplanted patients with SVP <14 mmHg had 100% 1-year survival, while non-transplanted patients with PCWP <15 mmHg also had 100% 1-year survival; and (5) at 10 years, all waitlisted/declined patients with PCWP ≥17 mmHg had died, whereas all transplanted/deferred patients with SVP <10 mmHg were alive. Tables 1–2 and Figures 1–2 were used directly for this extraction.

